WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.932
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:08.688
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit one: Epistemological Frameworks. Today you will argue about knowledge using abstract noun clauses.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.031
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:24.977
Rationalism.

7
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:30.700
Rationalism means the view that reason, not experience, is the main source of knowledge.

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:35.524
Descartes defended rationalism by doubting everything the senses report.

9
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:38.920
Your turn. Say: Rationalism.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:42.849
Rationalism.

11
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:44.863
Empiricism.

12
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.264
Empiricism means the view that knowledge comes mainly from sensory experience.

13
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.185
Empiricism holds that the mind begins as a blank slate.

14
00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:57.470
Your turn. Say: Empiricism.

15
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:01.683
Empiricism.

16
00:01:02.417 --> 00:01:03.534
A priori.

17
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:08.303
A priori means known through reason alone, before any experience.

18
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:12.182
Mathematical truths are often called a priori knowledge.

19
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:15.587
Your turn. Say: A priori.

20
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.539
A priori.

21
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.691
A posteriori.

22
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:26.529
A posteriori means known only through experience and observation.

23
00:01:27.117 --> 00:01:31.508
That water boils at 100 degrees is a posteriori knowledge.

24
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:34.920
Your turn. Say: A posteriori.

25
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:39.557
A posteriori.

26
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:41.515
Foundationalism.

27
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:47.024
Foundationalism means the theory that knowledge rests on basic, self-evident beliefs.

28
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:50.835
Foundationalism builds knowledge like a house on firm ground.

29
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.474
Your turn. Say: Foundationalism.

30
00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:58.601
Foundationalism.

31
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.490
Coherentism.

32
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:06.443
Coherentism means the theory that beliefs are justified by fitting together as a system.

33
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.501
Coherentism compares knowledge to a web, not a pyramid.

34
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:14.094
Your turn. Say: Coherentism.

35
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:18.213
Coherentism.

36
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.880
Epistemic justification.

37
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:26.588
Epistemic justification means the reasons that make a belief rationally acceptable.

38
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:31.017
Epistemic justification separates knowledge from lucky guesses.

39
00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:35.418
Your turn. Say: Epistemic justification.

40
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:40.359
Epistemic justification.

41
00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:42.594
Skeptical inquiry.

42
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:47.893
Skeptical inquiry means systematic questioning of claims before accepting them.

43
00:02:48.483 --> 00:02:51.923
Skeptical inquiry protected the lab from a flawed result.

44
00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:55.332
Your turn. Say: Skeptical inquiry.

45
00:02:58.167 --> 00:02:59.946
Skeptical inquiry.

46
00:03:00.683 --> 00:03:01.975
Ontological.

47
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:06.597
Ontological means concerning the nature of existence and being.

48
00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:10.634
The debate is ontological: does the number seven exist at all?

49
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.949
Your turn. Say: Ontological.

50
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.987
Ontological.

51
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:19.809
Inference.

52
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:24.256
Inference means a conclusion reached by reasoning from evidence.

53
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.185
From the wet streets, she drew the inference that it had rained.

54
00:03:28.783 --> 00:03:31.308
Your turn. Say: Inference.

55
00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:35.237
Inference.

56
00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:39.313
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:45.566
Visal, my professor claims we can't be certain of anything. Is that really where philosophy ends?

58
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:55.049
Not necessarily. Whether knowledge is justified depends on one's epistemic framework, and frameworks differ greatly.

59
00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.011
Differ how? Surely evidence is evidence.

60
00:04:00.117 --> 00:04:12.394
That evidence is neutral is itself a contested claim. What counts as proof varies between rationalists, who trust reason, and empiricists, who trust experience.

61
00:04:13.483 --> 00:04:14.960
So who is right?

62
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:25.134
That is precisely what skeptical inquiry asks. Whether either camp has secure foundations is the question each must answer for itself.

63
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.974
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:28.817 --> 00:04:32.755
Grammar focus: Abstract philosophical subordination.

65
00:04:33.055 --> 00:04:34.323
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:35.017 --> 00:04:38.769
Whether knowledge is justified depends on one's epistemic framework.

67
00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:43.192
That experience shapes belief is a central empiricist claim.

68
00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:48.103
What counts as evidence varies between philosophical traditions.

69
00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:57.103
At C2, a whole clause can be the subject: 'Whether…', 'That…', 'What…'. The verb that follows is singular and agrees with the entire clause, not its last noun.

70
00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.105
Be careful! Don't say: Is knowledge justified depends on the framework.

71
00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:07.738
Say: Whether knowledge is justified depends on the framework.

72
00:05:08.038 --> 00:05:10.084
Use 'whether' to make a noun clause.

73
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.680
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:15.712
What Can We Know?.

75
00:05:16.012 --> 00:05:21.622
Epistemology asks what knowledge is, how it is acquired and what justifies us in claiming it.

76
00:05:22.633 --> 00:05:31.390
Rationalists such as Descartes argued that reason alone yields a priori truths, whereas empiricists such as Locke insisted that all knowledge originates in experience.

77
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:41.052
Foundationalism seeks secure basic beliefs on which everything else rests, while coherentism holds that beliefs are justified by how well they fit together.

78
00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:49.825
Whether any framework escapes skeptical inquiry remains disputed, yet the questioning itself sharpens our standards of justification.

79
00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:56.896
What we call knowledge, then, may be less a possession than a continuing practice of testing our inferences.

80
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.897
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.574
blank knowledge is justified depends on the framework.

82
00:06:05.874 --> 00:06:09.492
A, Whether. B, Which. C, Despite.

83
00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:14.777
The answer is A: Whether.

84
00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:18.508
Knowledge gained through experience is called blank.

85
00:06:18.808 --> 00:06:23.774
A, a priori. B, ontological. C, a posteriori.

86
00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:30.029
The answer is C: a posteriori.

87
00:06:31.183 --> 00:06:34.251
The view that reason is the main source of knowledge is blank.

88
00:06:34.551 --> 00:06:38.971
A, rationalism. B, empiricism. C, coherentism.

89
00:06:42.533 --> 00:06:45.113
The answer is A: rationalism.

90
00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:47.994
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:48.294 --> 00:06:56.104
A, That evidence is neutral are contested.. B, That evidence is neutral is contested.. C, That evidence neutral is contested..

92
00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:03.535
The answer is B: That evidence is neutral is contested..

93
00:07:04.683 --> 00:07:08.889
According to the reading, coherentism holds that beliefs are justified by…

94
00:07:09.189 --> 00:07:14.537
A, secure foundations. B, sensory data. C, how well they fit together.

95
00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.889
The answer is C: how well they fit together.

96
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.553
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:26.417 --> 00:07:29.554
Do you trust reason or experience more as a source of knowledge?

98
00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:47.815
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:48.115 --> 00:08:18.303
I trust experience more, although I recognise that reason organises it. Whether a claim is true seems to me best settled by testing it against observation, because pure reasoning can drift away from reality. Mathematics is a priori, yet even mathematicians check their conjectures with examples. What convinces me most is a belief that survives repeated tests. Still, I accept that reason is needed to interpret what experience gives us.

100
00:08:18.603 --> 00:08:20.219
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:21.117 --> 00:08:26.114
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract philosophical subordination.

102
00:08:26.414 --> 00:08:31.354
Today's value is reasoned judgment. Weigh the evidence before you decide what to believe.

103
00:08:31.654 --> 00:08:33.900
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

104
00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.049
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

